---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import Byline from "@components/Byline.astro";
import RelatedTools from "@components/RelatedTools.astro";
import Figure from "@components/Figure.astro";
// PC에서 먼저 만든 두 장(MontyGame·MontyProbability)과 클라우드에서 만든 세 장을
// 2026-09-10에 합쳤다. 설정 → 단계 → 세 경우 → 확률 분포 → 문이 늘어나면 순서다.
import Probability from "./_fig/Probability.astro";
import TheQuestion from "./_fig/TheQuestion.astro";
import GameSteps from "./_fig/GameSteps.astro";
import ThreeCases from "./_fig/ThreeCases.astro";
import { tools_read_category } from "@data/tools.js";

// 등급·검수일은 Byline이 tools.js에서 읽는다. 페이지가 다시 말하지 않는다.
const SLUG = "monty-hall-n-doors";
const CATEGORY = "chance";
const cat = tools_read_category(CATEGORY);

// 2026-09-25 사실검사 반영 — 왜 이렇게 적혀 있는지(같은 곳을 되돌리지 않게):
//  · 셀빈 1975년 두 글은 논문이 아니라 편지란(Letters to the Editor)의 편지다.
//    두 DOI의 Crossref 레코드 제목이 둘 다 "Letters to the Editor"이고 쪽수는
//    67–71 / 131–134다(audit/sources/selvin-1975-letters-american-statistician.md).
//    그래서 제목 뒤에 (letter to the editor)를 달고, 첫 글은 셀빈의 편지가 실린
//    p. 67을 적은 뒤 편지란이 p. 71까지 간다고 밝힌다(67–71은 여러 편지의 쪽이다).
//    둘째 글도 이름이 처음 인쇄된 p. 134를 적는다.
//  · Ferguson 귀속에서 "commonly"를 뺐다 — 널리 그렇게 한다는 것을 받치는 출처가
//    위키백과 한 계열뿐이었다. 그래서 누구인지(the Wikipedia article)를 이름으로 적는다.
//  · "The formula does not care how the host picks the losing doors"에는 전제가
//    빠져 있었다. 무작위로 바꿀 때만 참이다 — 최소번호 습관 호스트에서 열린 문을
//    읽으면 N=4·K=1에 50%가 되고, 이 페이지가 아래에서 그 50%를 따로 적는다
//    (variants.test.js "N=4, K=1에서 정확히 50%다").

// 브레드크럼 이름. 화면과 구조화 데이터가 **같은 상수 하나**를 읽는다.
// 문자열을 두 번 적으면 갈라지고, 갈라지면 마크업이 화면과 다른 것을 말한다.
const CRUMB = "Monty Hall with N doors";
---

<Base
  title="Monty Hall with N doors: does switching still win?"
  description="Move the door count and the number of doors the host opens. The model's switching advantage runs from 99x down to barely 1x. Exact probabilities plus a simulation."
  category={CATEGORY}
  crumbTool={true}
  crumbLabel={CRUMB}
>
  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>{" "}
    <a href={cat.href}>{cat.name}</a> <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>{" "}
    {CRUMB}
  </nav>

  <h1>Monty Hall with N doors: does switching still win?</h1>

  <p class="deck">
    Three doors. You pick one. The host opens an empty one and offers a swap.
    Should you take it? And does the answer hold with a hundred doors?
  </p>

  <Byline slug={SLUG} />

  <Figure variant="lede" caption="In this model, the host never opens the door you picked.">
    <TheQuestion />
  </Figure>

  <p class="lead">
    Three doors. One hides a car. Two hide goats. In 1990 Marilyn vos Savant
    answered this in her <em>Parade</em> column. Swap, she said, and you win two
    games out of three. Stay and you win one. Thousands of letters told her she was
    wrong. Many came from readers with doctorates.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    Five years later Andrew Vazsonyi put the problem to the mathematician Paul
    Erdős. A decision tree did not convince him. Watching a simulation did.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    <span class="lead-turn">So this page plays the game before it argues.</span> Two
    sliders set the game: how many doors, and how many doors the host opens. A
    third sets how many games to play.
    Move the first two. They pull the answer in opposite directions.
  </p>

  <div id="sliders" data-widget="monty-hall-n-doors">
    <noscript>
      <p>
        The simulator, its chart and table need JavaScript. The exact numbers
        follow from the formula and the hand count below.
      </p>
    </noscript>
  </div>

  <h2 data-block="reversal">Adding doors alone does not help. Opening them does.</h2>

  <p>
    The first slider sets how many doors the game starts with. The second sets how
    many the host opens before the swap. The working below calls them N and K.
  </p>

  <Figure caption="In this model the order is fixed: you pick, the host opens, then the switch is offered.">
    <GameSteps />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    <strong>Three doors, one opened.</strong> That is the 1990 setting, and the
    model gives the famous answer. Staying wins one game in three. Swapping wins
    two. Swapping wins twice as often. Two closed doors do not mean even odds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now open more. At 100 doors with 98 opened, swapping wins 99 games in 100.
    Staying wins 1. The gap is enormous.
  </p>

  <Figure caption="In this model, the car's three placements decide every outcome.">
    <ThreeCases />
  </Figure>


  <p>
    Here is the surprise. Keep the 100 doors, but let the host open only one. Press
    the <strong>100 doors, one opened</strong> button and watch. Swapping now wins
    1.01 games for every 1 the staying player wins. Almost the whole advantage is
    gone — at a hundred doors.
  </p>

  <p>
    So more doors alone do not help. What helps is the host opening them. Everything you
    did not pick is one big share of the odds, and the host never throws that share
    away. He piles it onto fewer and fewer doors. Open one door out of a hundred
    and the share barely moves. Open 98 and all of it lands on the one door
    left.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model puts the advantage at the doors you did not pick, divided by the
    doors you could still swap to — <strong>(N−1)/R</strong> in the working below.
    Add doors but open no more of them, and both numbers grow by one. The
    advantage then sinks toward 1. The widget above shows all of this. Move the
    sliders and watch the Switch and Stay pairs of lines and the advantage figure.
    Here is how to read it.
  </p>

  <section class="howto" aria-labelledby="howto-title">
    <h2 data-block="howto" id="howto-title">How to read what you are looking at</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>The chart shows the share of games won so far.</strong> Left to right
      is games played, on a logarithmic axis. Marks sit at powers of ten where there
      is room, and the last mark is the number of games played. That is how a million
      games still fit on screen.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each strategy gets two lines in one colour. The <strong>solid line</strong> is
      the score so far in the played games. It jumps about early and settles later.
      The <strong>dashed line</strong> is the exact answer, worked out in advance.
      When the solid line settles onto the dashed one, the games and the maths
      agree.
    </p>

    <p>
      The up-and-down axis changes range to fit the sliders. At 100 doors with one
      opened, both exact lines sit near 1%. So read the labels before you compare two
      settings. The table below the chart drops the played games. It fixes one
      rule — the host opens every door but two, yours and one other — so you can
      compare door counts at that rule.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="math" aria-labelledby="math-title">
    <h2 data-block="math" class="math-title" id="math-title">Why the advantage is (N−1)/R</h2>

    <p>
      The first pick carries no information, and the host never opens the player's
      door — so that door's probability is fixed right there, at 1/N. In this model
      the host picks at random among the doors he may open. So nothing he does
      afterward moves it. That is why <strong>K never appears in
      P<sub>stay</sub></strong> below. Each door he opens only shares the rest among
      the doors you did not pick. So the second slider changes P<sub>switch</sub>{" "}
      and leaves P<sub>stay</sub> alone.
    </p>

    <Probability />

    <div class="math-eq">
      <span>P<sub>stay</sub></span><span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="sr-only">(</span><span class="frac-num">1</span><span class="sr-only">) over (</span><span class="frac-den">N</span><span class="sr-only">)</span></span>
    </div>

    <div class="math-eq">
      <span>P<sub>switch</sub></span><span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="sr-only">(</span><span class="frac-num">N − 1</span><span class="sr-only">) over (</span><span class="frac-den">N</span><span class="sr-only">)</span></span>
      <span class="op">×</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="sr-only">(</span><span class="frac-num">1</span><span class="sr-only">) over (</span><span class="frac-den">R</span><span class="sr-only">)</span></span>
      <span class="op">=</span>
      <span class="frac"><span class="sr-only">(</span><span class="frac-num">N − 1</span><span class="sr-only">) over (</span><span class="frac-den">N (N − 1 − K)</span><span class="sr-only">)</span></span>
    </div>

    <dl class="math-where">
      <dt>N</dt><dd>how many doors the game starts with, 3 to 100 — the first slider</dd>
      <dt>K</dt><dd>how many the host opens, 1 to N − 2 — the second slider</dd>
      <dt>R</dt><dd>doors you could swap to, R = N − 1 − K</dd>
    </dl>

    <div class="math-steps-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Derivation steps">
    <table class="math-steps">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">Expression</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>First pick holds the prize</td>
          <td>1 / N</td>
          <td>Uniform placement, no information yet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>First pick is wrong</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / N</td>
          <td>Complement of row 1 — one of the N − 1 unpicked doors</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Host removes K losing doors</td>
          <td>R = N − 1 − K</td>
          <td>Host opens K of those, all losing, leaving R closed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Switch lands on the prize</td>
          <td>1 / R</td>
          <td>The prize is one of the R survivors</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="math-result">
          <td>Switching wins</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / (N · R)</td>
          <td>Row 2 × row 4</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="math-result">
          <td>Advantage over staying</td>
          <td>(N − 1) / R</td>
          <td>P<sub>switch</sub> ÷ P<sub>stay</sub></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <p class="math-check">
      <strong>Two checks.</strong> At N=3, K=1: R=1, so switching is 2/3 and
      staying 1/3, summing to 1. The same holds whenever the host opens every
      door except yours and one other (K = N−2, R = 1): two doors remain, and switching is
      (N−1)/N.
    </p>

    <p>
      The formula does not care <em>how</em> the host picks the losing doors — so
      long as you swap at random, ignoring which door he opened. Under
      any such rule, a wrong first pick leaves the prize among the R closed doors.
      A random swap finds it 1 time in R. Count a small case by hand and the
      formula gets easier to trust.
    </p>
  </section>
  <h2 data-block="byhand">Four doors, counted by hand</h2>

  <p>
    Take four doors and one opened. You pick door 1. Staying wins whenever the prize
    is behind door 1. That happens in 1 game out of 4, so staying wins{" "}
    <strong>25.0%</strong> of the time.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the other 3 games your pick is wrong. The prize hides behind door 2, 3 or 4.
    The host opens one empty door from that group, never the prize. Two doors stay
    shut, and the prize sits behind one of them. So a wrong first pick becomes a win
    half the time: <code>(3/4) × (1/2) = 3/8 = 37.5%</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Swapping beats staying, 37.5% to 25.0%. But that is far below the 66.7% of the
    three-door game. The games left over are the ones where the swap lands on the
    wrong door of the two. All of it rests on four rules.
  </p>

  <h2 data-block="assumptions">Four rules, all load-bearing</h2>

  <p>
    Every game on this page follows these four. Drop any one and the numbers above
    stop meaning what they say.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>The model hides one prize behind a random door.</li>
    <li>The player picks one door.</li>
    <li>
      The host knows the prize door. He opens K doors, never yours and never the
      prize.
    </li>
    <li>
      The player may swap to one of the R doors still closed. With this host they
      are all alike, so which one does not matter.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    <strong>The played games.</strong> The page plays many random games. It assumes
    the same even odds the maths does. A separate test counts every arrangement
    of three to eight doors exactly. Under this page's host rule, the counts match
    the formula. A seeded generator makes the randomness, not{" "}
    <code>Math.random</code>. The seed rides in the URL, so the curve repeats.
  </p>

  <p class="note"><a href="#sliders">Back to the sliders ↑</a></p>

  <h2 data-block="limits">A different host can break the numbers here</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The host's rule.</strong> Say the host opens doors at random. Count only
    the games where he misses the prize, and the arithmetic ties swapping with
    staying.
    Say he offers the swap only to players who are already right. Swapping then loses
    every game. On screen all three hosts look the same.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>A host with a habit.</strong> This model's host picks at random among
    the empty doors he may open. A host who always opens the lowest empty door
    leaks a clue through <em>which</em> door he opens. Counted by hand, a player
    who reads that habit wins 50%, not 37.5%. That is four doors with one opened,
    worked by hand.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Too small to see.</strong> At 100 doors with one opened, the true gap is
    about 1 game in 10,000. Even a million played games wobble by about that much,
    and a million is the slider's limit. On the chart the two exact lines nearly
    touch. The legend under the chart says when they are too close to separate, and
    the advantage figure carries the difference instead.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Two limits of the display.</strong> Both strategies are scored on the same
    games, and no game can be won both ways — with more than one door left to swap
    into, a game can be lost both ways. So the two solid curves are tied together,
    not independent.
    The banner changes wording at lines I chose — 2.00× and 1.10×. No statistical
    test sets them. Each solid curve settles onto its dashed line given
    enough games, and at the closest settings a million is not enough. Settling is
    not explaining.
  </p>

  <p class="note"><a href="#sliders">Back to the sliders ↑</a></p>

  <h2 data-block="closing">The simulation agreed. It did not explain.</h2>

  <p>
    Vazsonyi's account of the Erdős conversation has three steps, and they did not
    arrive together. The decision tree did not persuade him. A simulation did. The
    reason why came days later, and from someone else — Ron Graham.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first two steps are on the same screen above. Neither line is offered as
    proof of the other — they are two routes to the same number, and the only claim
    being made is that they meet.
  </p>

  <p>
    The third step is the one a page cannot hand over. Seeing swapping win two games
    in three is not the same as seeing <em>why</em>. Your door was locked at 1 in 3
    the moment you touched it. The host may not open it, and this model's host
    picks at random, so nothing he does can change it. The hand count above is the closest this page gets.
  </p>

  <p>
    The arithmetic is older than the game show. Martin Gardner published an equivalent
    problem in <em>Scientific American</em> in 1959. It is known as the Three Prisoners
    Problem: three condemned men, one of them to be pardoned, and a warden who names
    one of the other two who will be executed — flipping a coin when both will be.
    Selvin's boxes came just over fifteen years later.
  </p>

  <h2 data-block="about">About this page</h2>

  <p class="note">
    The exact probabilities come from the formula above. <a href="/about">How I
    check things</a>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Sources.</strong></p>

  <ul class="sources">
    <li>
      Steve Selvin posed the problem in "A Problem in Probability" (letter to the
      editor), <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1975.10479121"><em>The
      American Statistician</em> 29(1), February 1975, p. 67</a>, in a Letters to
      the Editor section running to p. 71 — with three boxes, not doors.
    </li>
    <li>
      He returned to it in "On the Monty Hall Problem" (letter to the editor), <a
      href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1975.10477398">29(3), August 1975,
      p. 134</a>, the earliest known appearance of that name in print.
    </li>
    <li>
      The N-door generalisation follows from the same argument; the Wikipedia
      article on the problem credits it to D. L. Ferguson. The original could not
      be consulted.
    </li>
    <li>
      The problem reached a wide audience through Marilyn vos Savant's "Ask
      Marilyn" column in <em>Parade</em>, 9 September 1990; the reader response
      described above is from John Tierney's report in <em>The New York Times</em>,
      21 July 1991.
    </li>
    <li>The Erdős episode is Andrew Vazsonyi's own account of it, published in <em>Decision Line</em>, December/January 1999, pp. 17–19.</li>
    <li>
      The Three Prisoners Problem is in Martin Gardner's "Mathematical Games"
      column, <a
      href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/mathematical-games-1959-10/"><em>Scientific
      American</em> 201(4), October 1959, p. 174</a>, which states the problem and
      leaves the analysis to the next issue. The warden's rule above is the
      asking prisoner's own request:
      "And if I'm to be pardoned, flip a coin to decide whether to name B or C."
    </li>
    <li>
      Jason Rosenhouse's <a
      href="https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195367898.001.0001"><em>The Monty
      Hall Problem</em></a> (Oxford University Press, 2009) covers the variants.
    </li>
  </ul>


  <RelatedTools slug={SLUG} />

  <script>
    import "@widgets/monty-hall-n-doors/widget.js";
  </script>
</Base>
